{
  "_comment": "Independent gold record for all 97 tickets in fixtures/tickets.json, read only by eval/ (see eval/gold.py) -- never by agent/, which is enforced by tests/test_gold_isolation.py. `defective` is the one hand-authored fact: read from the customer message alone, by a human reader who did not consult agent/agent.py::_has or the _DEFECTIVE lexicon, and independent of anything the agent itself extracts. The other eight keys are order-derived and computed mechanically, exactly as services_mock/order_api.py::order_facts computes them against services_mock/data.py::TODAY -- see tests/test_gold_facts.py::test_order_derived_facts_match_order_api for the programmatic proof. `order_found` has a third state, `null`, distinct from `false` (no order exists) and `true` (found): it means the customer's email matches more than one order and no single order can supply the other seven facts -- UN-13, ASK-02, HO-ASK-02. `days_since_delivery` is `null` whenever the order has not been delivered (data.days_since(None)), never coerced to zero. `final_sale` is `null` on ORD-6008 (UN-08, HO-UN-07), preserving a genuine data gap rather than guessing. ORD-8001 (ASK-01, HO-ASK-01) has two items; order_facts reads items[0] only, so these order-derived facts describe the Cotton Hoodie even though the ticket does not pin down which item the customer means -- see .superpowers/sdd/IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN-multilingual-grounding-gate/gold-facts-draft.md section 5. `defective` for the 17 fault-tier tickets (FA-*, HO-FA-*) is copied verbatim from fixtures/tickets.json's expected.gold_defective, which tests/test_agent.py::test_gold_defective_consistent_with_licensed_outcome already re-derives against live kb.licensed_outcome() -- eval/gold.py resolves `defective` from that field directly rather than from this copy, so this file's copy is a redundant, test-checked mirror, not a second source of truth. THE false-VS-null RULE for every other `defective`: `false` requires the message to supply a complete non-defect reason for the return -- an explicit works-fine statement, an unwanted gift, a change of mind, or a fit/style/no-longer-want remark -- treated as a positive indication the item is not being returned for a fault. `null` covers everything short of that: a bare return/status/eligibility request with no reason, a claim about something other than the item's condition (billing, account security, support quality, timing, loyalty), or pure dissatisfaction/anger with no assertion about function. This is the more permissive reading flagged in gold-facts-draft.md section 6 item 1 -- a stricter rule (only an explicit works-fine statement counts) would move CR-10, AD-08, HO-CR-02, HO-CR-03, HO-UN-07 and HO-AD-06 from `false` to `null`. The choice is currently outcome-inert: RET-020 is the only rule reading `defective` and it tests `== True`, so `false` and `null` license the same outcome under every other rule in kb/rules.json today.",
